      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "In many western countries, the traditional 6 week check is a routine medical review of a mother and her baby 6 weeks after the birth. In Australia, general practitioners perform the majority of postpartum assessments."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "This article discusses some of the common issues that concern women following the birth of a baby, and the medical conditions GPs need to address in the postpartum period."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "DISCUSSION",
          "content": "The research literature does not support any benefit of a 6 week check. However, clinical evidence, our expertise as GPs, and our personal experiences, clearly indicate that the postpartum period is a time of tremendous change, increased health problems, and emotional upheaval for new parents. General practitioners are in an ideal position to assist families during this period and may consider a sooner rather than later, proactive rather than reactive, approach to postpartum care."
        }
      ]},
